**Negative space rule: emptiness is intentional.** A card with content only in the bottom-right corner and whitespace everywhere else is confident design, not missing content.

## Composition Pattern 1: Dense Information Grid

The workhorse. 3 or 4 even columns, ALL filled with prose + bold sub-headers. 150-300 words. Card heading uses H3 (NOT H1 — H1 is reserved for dramatic/sparse patterns).

### Even 3-col prose (33/33/34)
Three equal columns, each with heading + 2-3 paragraphs. Good for tier comparison, 3-step process, three market analyses. vertical-align="start" or "end".

### Even 4-col grid (25/25/25/25)
Four equal columns for team members, quarterly data, feature comparison. Each column: heading + 2-3 lines. Very dense, newspaper-like. vertical-align="start" or "end".

### Dense 2-col editorial (50/50)
Both columns dense prose with bold sub-headers. Magazine-spread feel. One column might have a bordered callout box. vertical-align="start" or "end".

### Dense 3-col with image (33/33/34)
2 prose columns + 1 image column. Image ALONE in its column. Even split maintained. vertical-align="start" or "end".

### Dense 3-col with header row
Full-width H3 heading above, then 3 columns of content below. Good for section openers. vertical-align="start".

## Composition Pattern 2: Asymmetric Information Card

2 equal-width columns with DRAMATICALLY different content density. The contrast IS the design. 50-80 words prose + 3-8 word headline.

### Prose-left, headline-right (50/50)
Left column: 1-2 paragraphs of prose. Right column: single H1, right-aligned, bottom-anchored. The headline is the card's thesis — dramatic because of its ISOLATION in an otherwise empty column. vertical-align="end".

### Headline-left, prose-right (50/50)
Inverted: H1 in left column (left-aligned, bottom-anchored), prose in right column. Use for variety across a deck. vertical-align="end".

The key: one column is DENSE, the other is SPARSE. Equal width, unequal content. This asymmetry is the most distinctively Swiss composition pattern.

## Composition Pattern 3: Single Column Positioned

Full-width card with content positioned deliberately. Uses negative space as design element. 30-60 words.

### Bottom-right positioned
Content pushed to bottom-right. H1 statement + optional P metadata. vertical-align="end", text-align="right". Whitespace above and left is intentional.

### Bottom-left positioned
Content pushed to bottom-left. Same structure, opposite corner. vertical-align="end", text-align="left".

### With background image
image-layout="behind" with atmospheric background. H1 text overlay, bottom-anchored. Text uses white color spans for legibility. Dramatic, cinematic.

### With accent background
Solid dark background (#1A1A1A or brand color). H1 in contrasting color. Bottom-anchored. Visual punctuation.

Use for: title cards, closing cards, mid-deck insight moments. The positioning IS the design.

## Composition Pattern 4: Data + Context

2 even columns with distinct roles. One column carries prose context, the other carries data. 60-120 words.

### Prose + stats (50/50)
Left: 1-2 paragraphs of prose context. Right: stats smart-layout or key numbers with labels. The data column is SPARSE — a few numbers, not wall-to-wall text. vertical-align="start" or "end".

### Prose + image (50/50)
Left: prose summary. Right: single image alone in its column. Even 50/50 split. Image is functional (product shot, diagram, headshot). vertical-align="start" or "end".

### Stats + prose (50/50)
Inverted: data/stats in left column, prose context in right. Use for variety.

