H1 anchors the card at the top — the card's thesis or section title. Below: 3 equal columns of supporting prose, each with its own H4 sub-header. The H1 is the headline; the grid delivers detail. vertical-align="start".

### H1 + 2-col supporting
Same concept with 2 columns below for simpler content. H1 provides the frame; columns fill in evidence. vertical-align="start".

## Composition Pattern 6: Pull-Quote Composition

One column is a large H1 (a key quote or insight). The other column is smaller supporting context. 40-80 words + 5-15 word quote.

### Quote-left, context-right (50/50)
Left: H1 with the quote, left-aligned. Right: P with supporting context — who said it, why it matters, or background data. vertical-align="end".

### Quote-right, context-left (50/50)
Inverted: prose context in left column, H1 quote right-aligned in right column. vertical-align="end".

Similar to asymmetric-info but the H1 is a QUOTE (someone's words or a key finding), not a thesis headline. The quote should be memorable, specific, and tied to the deck's argument.

## Composition Pattern 7: Image + Text

One column is a visual element (alone — nothing else in that column), the other is prose. Clean separation. 60-120 words + image/diagram query. The visual can be an IMG or a DIAGRAM.

