## Spatial Composition
Three equal columns (33/33/34), each densely filled. Left column: Capital Intensity heading with two paragraphs including financing gaps analysis. Middle column: Regulatory Risk heading with two paragraphs including compliance complexity details. Right column: Technical Barriers heading with two paragraphs including manufacturing bottlenecks analysis. All content top-anchored, left-aligned within each column.

## Card Content (HTML)
<h1>Sector Headwinds</h1>
    
    <h4>Capital Intensity</h4>
    <p>Hardware-heavy climate solutions require $50-200M in project finance before revenue generation. Manufacturing scale demands gigafactory investments averaging $2.8B for battery plants, $1.2B for solar module facilities. Working capital needs stress venture-backed companies as production ramps exceed 18-month timelines.</p>
    <p>**Financing Gaps** emerge in the $25-100M range where venture capital ends and project finance begins. Development capital markets remain underdeveloped for climate hardware. Asset-heavy business models struggle with traditional VC return expectations, creating systematic funding shortfalls for promising technologies approaching commercial scale.</p>
    
    <h4>Regulatory Risk</h4>
    <p>Policy uncertainty creates deployment friction across all climate sectors. IRA tax credit transferability rules remain unclear 18 months post-passage. Permitting timelines average 4.2 years for transmission projects, 2.8 years for utility-scale renewables. State-level policy reversals in Texas and Florida disrupted $8B in planned investments during 2024.</p>
    <p>**Compliance Complexity** increases with overlapping federal, state, and local requirements. Environmental review processes lack standardization across jurisdictions. Grid interconnection rules vary by ISO, creating developer confusion. Carbon accounting standards remain fragmented, complicating corporate procurement decisions and limiting market liquidity.</p>
    
    <h4>Technical Barriers</h4>
    <p>Materials science limitations in carbon capture constrain cost reduction below $150/ton CO2. Sorbent degradation requires replacement every 18-24 months. High-temperature industrial processes lack viable electrification pathways. Hydrogen storage density remains 3x below gasoline energy equivalent, limiting transportation applications.</p>
    <p>**Manufacturing Bottlenecks** persist in critical minerals processing. Lithium refining capacity trails demand by 340,000 tons annually. Rare earth element concentration in China creates supply chain vulnerabilities. Advanced materials like solid-state electrolytes require entirely new production infrastructure, delaying commercial deployment until 2028-2030.</p>

## Context
Deck intent: A systematic analysis of the climate tech landscape through the lens of Swiss design precision. Dense information grids carry the analytical weight, while asymmetric compositions highlight key market dynamics. Visual content focuses on clean architectural diagrams of technology systems and stark data presentations. The deck alternates between information-heavy analysis and dramatic thesis statements, creating a rhythm that mirrors the urgency and complexity of climate innovation.
Card instructions: Dense-grid: 3-column deep dive into sector challenges. Left column: '**Capital Intensity** Hardware-heavy climate solutions require...' Middle column: '**Regulatory Risk** Policy uncertainty creates deployment friction...' Right column: '**Technical Barriers** Materials science limitations in carbon capture...' Each column 2-3 paragraphs. The analytical backbone of sector headwinds.

## Art Direction (MUST follow)
This climate tech deck demands the precision of Swiss design to match the analytical rigor of the content. Every card commits to geometric clarity and information hierarchy, using the stark contrast between dense analytical grids and dramatic single-statement compositions to mirror the urgency of climate innovation. The register is outline-based and geometric throughout—outlineBoxes for parallel market segments, stats layouts for the critical financial data, and topLineText for structured opportunity vectors. No organic shapes or decorative elements; the content's complexity provides the visual interest through grid positioning and information density.

Background alternation creates analytical rhythm: predominantly white cards with selective dark backgrounds (#1A1A1A) on 1-2 key thesis cards to create dramatic pauses in the data flow. The single accent color—a muted blue (#2B4A6B)—appears only in the stats layouts for key financial figures and in the outline boxes for sector categorization, never overwhelming the monochromatic foundation. Visual content focuses exclusively on functional elements: clean architectural diagrams of energy systems positioned alone in dedicated columns, and stark data presentations that support the analytical narrative. The behind-image treatment appears only once, on a thesis card where the H1 statement overlays an atmospheric energy infrastructure photograph.

Intentional rule-breaking occurs in the pull-quote cards, where the dramatic H1 statements break left-alignment to anchor bottom-right, creating visual tension against the predominantly left-aligned grid system. However, never use centered text, never mix decorative smart layouts, and never compromise the even column splits that give this analytical content its structural integrity. The information density varies dramatically between cards—from 300-word dense grids carrying the market analysis to single-statement breather cards that let the most critical insights breathe—but every element serves the analytical argument, never the aesthetic.

## Allowed Variants (structural reference)
- text: topLineText, sideLineText
- boxes: outlineBoxes
- stats: stats
Max families per deck: 1
Use ONLY variants from the allowed list. The art direction above tells you WHICH to pick and WHEN.

## Image Layout
image-layout="blank". No section-level accent image. This guide supports inline visual content, but this particular card does not require any. Focus on prose content.

