## Spatial Composition
Three equal columns, each with H4 sub-header and 2-3 paragraphs below. Left column: Market Size analysis with geographic distribution. Middle column: Investment Flow analysis with corporate strategic investment breakdown. Right column: Policy Drivers analysis with compliance markets detail. All content top-anchored, left-aligned. Dense information grid with consistent column structure throughout.

## Card Content (HTML)
<h1>Market Fundamentals</h1>
    
    <h4>Market Size</h4>
    <p>The global climate tech market reached $1.2T in 2024, representing 18% growth from 2023. Energy storage dominates with $347B in deployed capital, followed by renewable generation at $298B. Carbon management technologies captured $89B, marking a 156% increase as corporate buyers embraced removal credits.</p>
    <p>**Geographic Distribution** North America commands 34% market share, driven by IRA incentives and corporate procurement. Europe holds 29%, accelerated by REPowerEU mandates. China's 28% share reflects manufacturing scale in solar and batteries, while emerging markets represent 9% but show fastest growth rates.</p>
    
    <h4>Investment Flow</h4>
    <p>Venture capital deployment shifted toward later-stage rounds as early technologies mature. Series B+ rounds captured 67% of $48B total climate VC in 2024, up from 52% in 2022. Hardware-intensive sectors like energy storage and industrial decarbonization attracted 71% of growth capital.</p>
    <p>**Corporate Strategic Investment** reached $23B, with utilities leading at $8.9B deployed. Oil majors contributed $6.2B, primarily in carbon capture and hydrogen. Tech companies allocated $4.1B toward grid software and demand response platforms. Family offices and sovereign wealth funds emerged as significant players, contributing $11B across all stages.</p>
    
    <h4>Policy Drivers</h4>
    <p>Regulatory frameworks in EU and US created $127B in direct climate tech incentives during 2024. The Inflation Reduction Act generated $89B in tax credit utilization, while EU's Green Deal Industrial Plan mobilized €38B in member state co-investment. China's carbon market expansion to petrochemicals and aviation added 2.1 billion tons of coverage.</p>
    <p>**Compliance Markets** Carbon pricing mechanisms now cover 23% of global emissions, up from 16% in 2021. California's cap-and-trade system reached $28/ton CO2, while EU ETS averaged €85/ton. Corporate voluntary commitments totaled 847 companies with science-based targets, driving $34B in procurement of climate solutions.</p>

## Context
Deck intent: A systematic analysis of the climate tech landscape through the lens of Swiss design precision. Dense information grids carry the analytical weight, while asymmetric compositions highlight key market dynamics. Visual content focuses on clean architectural diagrams of technology systems and stark data presentations. The deck alternates between information-heavy analysis and dramatic thesis statements, creating a rhythm that mirrors the urgency and complexity of climate innovation.
Card instructions: Dense-grid: 3-column analysis of climate tech market fundamentals. Left column: '**Market Size** The global climate tech market reached $1.2T in 2024...' Middle column: '**Investment Flow** Venture capital deployment shifted toward...' Right column: '**Policy Drivers** Regulatory frameworks in EU and US...' Each column 2-3 paragraphs with bold sub-headers. Comprehensive market overview.

## Art Direction (MUST follow)
This climate tech deck demands the precision of Swiss design to match the analytical rigor of the content. Every card commits to geometric clarity and information hierarchy, using the stark contrast between dense analytical grids and dramatic single-statement compositions to mirror the urgency of climate innovation. The register is outline-based and geometric throughout—outlineBoxes for parallel market segments, stats layouts for the critical financial data, and topLineText for structured opportunity vectors. No organic shapes or decorative elements; the content's complexity provides the visual interest through grid positioning and information density.

Background alternation creates analytical rhythm: predominantly white cards with selective dark backgrounds (#1A1A1A) on 1-2 key thesis cards to create dramatic pauses in the data flow. The single accent color—a muted blue (#2B4A6B)—appears only in the stats layouts for key financial figures and in the outline boxes for sector categorization, never overwhelming the monochromatic foundation. Visual content focuses exclusively on functional elements: clean architectural diagrams of energy systems positioned alone in dedicated columns, and stark data presentations that support the analytical narrative. The behind-image treatment appears only once, on a thesis card where the H1 statement overlays an atmospheric energy infrastructure photograph.

Intentional rule-breaking occurs in the pull-quote cards, where the dramatic H1 statements break left-alignment to anchor bottom-right, creating visual tension against the predominantly left-aligned grid system. However, never use centered text, never mix decorative smart layouts, and never compromise the even column splits that give this analytical content its structural integrity. The information density varies dramatically between cards—from 300-word dense grids carrying the market analysis to single-statement breather cards that let the most critical insights breathe—but every element serves the analytical argument, never the aesthetic.

## Allowed Variants (structural reference)
- text: topLineText, sideLineText
- boxes: outlineBoxes
- stats: stats
Max families per deck: 1
Use ONLY variants from the allowed list. The art direction above tells you WHICH to pick and WHEN.

